  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How do I make a word plural?",
      "a": "Most words just add s: cat, cats. Words ending in s, x, sh or ch add es, and a consonant plus y changes to ies.",
      "asks": [
        "Plural rules"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "When do I add es?",
      "a": "Add es when a word ends in s, x, z, sh or ch, like buses, foxes, dishes and churches. It adds an extra beat you can hear.",
      "asks": [
        "Foxes or foxs?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the plural of child?",
      "a": "Children. Child is an irregular plural, like mouse and mice, tooth and teeth, and man and men.",
      "asks": [
        "Irregular plurals"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the plural of baby?",
      "a": "Babies. When a word ends in a consonant and y, change the y to i and add es.",
      "asks": [
        "Y to ies rule"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is the plural of sheep still sheep?",
      "a": "Some animal words, like sheep, deer and fish, stay the same in the plural. It is a leftover from Old English.",
      "asks": [
        "Sheep plural"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "In Indonesian and Malay, doubling a noun can make it plural: buku-buku means books.",
    "Old English plurals like children, oxen and mice survive in modern English."
